WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.792
The history and the present are all intermingled into one.

00:00:03.792 --> 00:00:07.800
We know for a fact that laborers and enslaved people

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.480
preemancipation were living in these cabins,

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.260
but we also know they were used as guest

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:14.099
houses in more recent times, and then in

00:00:14.099 --> 00:00:15.780
some cases they would use as trash dumps

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:17.070
in even more recent times.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.640
So we get all of that trash and material

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.280
sort of mixed in together.

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:23.780
The beauty of it is that it allows us to

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:27.000
sort of look around and analyze the entire spectrum

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.160
of life on the estate.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.300
In the 1800's, enslaved Africans were brought into the

00:00:32.309 --> 00:00:34.460
ports of Christiansted on the Caribbean

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:37.600
island of St. Croix.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:40.460
About a hundred forty enslaved people were forced to

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:43.100
work on Estate Little Princess, a sugar plantation

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:45.900
and Rum factory.

00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:47.700
The project at the Estate Little princess really

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.380
began as a terrestrial archaeology component

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:53.320
to a Slave Wrecks Project, and the idea was that we

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.000
would have a terrestrial archaeology site,

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.920
and a maritime archaeology site

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:59.550
that could both speak to the slave trade from

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:01.180
different perspectives.

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:03.000
While the terrestrial archaeology has been

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.700
forthcoming, we haven't been so forthcoming in terms of

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.260
locating a shipwreck to tell that sort of

00:01:08.260 --> 00:01:10.820
maritime perspective.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.820
Overall it's been good because it's been providing new

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:16.480
information and new insight into ways that maritime

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:19.440
resources are actually used on terrestrial landscapes.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:22.100
So aspects such as coral mining that was

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.090
used for housing construction, and then

00:01:24.090 --> 00:01:26.220
also some of the shellfish and Whiting fish

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:29.660
of course that were used as provisions on the island.

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:31.980
Still trying to understand how this landscape

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:35.740
has changed over time  is one of my main questions.

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:39.150
The island itself is borderline on its ability to

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:42.300
provide enough water for that many plantations.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:45.580
Europeans who came here, along with others,

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:48.420
removed a lot of the surface vegetation so that they

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:50.760
could clear spaces to cultivate sugar.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:53.159
When all those trees are removed, then the natural

00:01:53.159 --> 00:01:57.220
soil deposition and also water regimes

00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:59.240
are changed across the entire island.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:01.940
Those kind of changes are visible on the landscape even

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:05.440
still, because even though there's not sugar widespread

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:08.459
cultivation on Island, it's changed the possible

00:02:08.459 --> 00:02:10.820
plants that could actually live there.

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:12.300
It really allows us to think about how

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:15.489
enslaved Africans were using plant material, and that's

00:02:15.489 --> 00:02:18.240
important because in the archaeological record a lot of

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:22.220
those don't don't appear, mainly because they go away.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:24.280
But if we have access to the plants that are still

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:26.360
growing today, we can begin to think

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:27.879
about how these plants may have been

00:02:27.879 --> 00:02:31.560
used by historic populations in the past.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.620
Fewer people are talking about actually environmental

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.920
reparations, and what does it look like when we talk

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:37.920
about reparations in a context that has been

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.209
completely decimated by the slave trade.

00:02:40.209 --> 00:02:41.340
and by slavery.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:44.100
We're looking at increased soil erosion as a result of

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:45.780
these clear-cutting of forests.

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:47.950
We're looking at increased wave turbidity at the

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:49.200
destruction of these coral reefs ,

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.919
and that's something that doesn't just grow

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.020
back over a ten, 20 or even 50 years

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:56.940
In addition to surveying the environment,

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:59.660
researchers are speaking with the community, and using

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:02.800
historical records to better understand the occupation of

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:05.060
enslaved people on Estate Little Princess.

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:10.380
We don't have a 100% clear understanding of the full

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.020
range of occupation.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:15.700
We know that the first parcel that became Estate Little

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:17.319
Princess was

00:03:17.319 --> 00:03:20.220
purchased in 1749 and that it was only 11 or 13 acres.

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:23.180
Right now it's more than 30 acres.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:26.917
So those maps show that up to 30 cabins existed

00:03:26.917 --> 00:03:28.280
in the area where we're excavating.

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:31.400
Of course we've only found the remains of four.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:32.740
By the 60s, there was a really sustained effort for

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:38.380
individuals to stop living in those cabins unless they

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:42.120
were working for the property owner.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:45.440
From our perspective today it seems improbable

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:48.220
or illogical to stay in the place where slavery was

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:51.326
taking place, but for folks who were freed they didn't

00:03:51.326 --> 00:03:53.320
have a whole lot of options.

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:56.640
There weren't guarantees that if they left St. Croix

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:58.920
they were gonna find a better life somewhere else.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:01.870
Focusing in on the humanity of the

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:05.580
enslaved people is central to their research goals.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:08.140
There's a way that people are obsessed,

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:11.700
that scholars have become obsessed with numbers.

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:14.600
And if we can pinpoint the exact amount of people

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:16.660
that came from this place this place or

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:21.506
this place, if we had the exact amount of glass of

00:04:21.506 --> 00:04:24.220
ceramic of fauna, if we could compile these in these

00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:28.270
databases, and it can give us this greater image of what

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:30.380
enslaved life was like.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:33.460
But ultimately, if we're just talking about things

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:37.400
and we actually miss the human aspect of it,

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:39.680
it can be really dangerous.

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:43.700
So thinking about agency is this way of ensuring that

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:46.540
we're talking about humans, and that we're thinking

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:50.399
about various levels of agency, all under

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:52.540
the umbrella of enslavement.

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:56.480
So very complex questions and explorations,

00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:00.260
but certainly worth diving into to really say out loud that

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:02.420
although these people were enslaved,

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:04.460
that they actually moved through this world,

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:06.080
and made choices under that regime

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:07.780
about how they lived their lives,

00:05:07.780 --> 00:05:10.080
about how they built community with each other,

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:12.340
about how they loved themselves and others.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.840
The artifacts on St. Croix help tell these

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:19.420
stories of agency and resilience.

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:22.700
We recovered a perforated shell here at the site,

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:24.020
and we've recovered a number of

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:26.440
smaller finds that could have been used

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:29.200
in adornment practices, like glass beads,

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:32.640
bone buttons, ceramic buttons, hook and eye fasteners.

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:35.940
So these small finds that speak to these larger dynamics

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:38.160
of dress at the site.

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:40.140
The fact that we're finding not just porcelain,

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:43.340
but hand-painted porcelain at Estate Little Princess,

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:46.680
tells something about access, and agency and the

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:49.320
movement of goods between communities

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:50.420
on this island.

00:05:50.420 --> 00:05:52.240
So even though they're living
inland,

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:53.720
they're nowhere near the town,

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:56.720
They're able to participate in the economy in a different

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:59.620
way than we might have thought of before.

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:03.219
They are not just there waiting for someone to

00:06:03.219 --> 00:06:05.360
break something so they can get a hand-me-down,

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:08.540
they are actively procuring their own wares.

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:12.520
As a purification, they would have a limestone block,

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:15.080
and they'd perch that somewhere and the

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:17.349
limestone block has properties that is a

00:06:17.349 --> 00:06:20.960
natural purifier, so they literally run water through the

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:24.040
stone, and then it would drip through the limestone into

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:27.240
a large storage vessel--the Afro-crucian ware.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:28.900
So they're taking that same pottery,

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:31.210
but they're literally creating a system

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:34.120
to purify their own water, because they have been

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:37.020
blocked from access to cisterns that the Europeans

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:39.789
are mainly using, and they are relegated to using

00:06:39.789 --> 00:06:43.440
these free guts, that are highly contaminated because

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:47.620
everyone's using it for water, for cleaning, for livestock,

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:51.480
for bathing, for any any purpose you can imagine.

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:55.420
They came up with a way to solve that themselves.

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:58.360
We're really interested in really understanding what

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:01.140
they went, through and what they survived,

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:04.560
what they see as their expression,

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:07.480
their identity, their culture, and their community

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:09.540
Beyond just looking at the artifacts,

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:10.569
beyond just looking at that

00:07:10.569 --> 00:07:12.360
status of being enslaved.

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:15.759
We want to be able to tell the most complete story we

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:19.060
can, with the bits of pieces that we have to tell that.

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:28.040
you

